## Why This Is Asked

This question tests end-to-end real-time data engineering competencies on GCP, including multi-tenant isolation, dynamic schema evolution, data contracts, and rollback semantics, plus cross-cloud replication and testing strategies.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time ingestion with Pub/Sub and Dataflow templates
- Per-tenant isolation using partitioned BigQuery datasets
- Central schema registry in Data Catalog for dynamic evolution
- End-to-end lineage and data contracts
- 24-hour per-tenant rollback via BigQuery time travel
- Cross-region replication to Snowflake via Snowpipe

## Code Example

```java
// Dataflow pipeline with tenant isolation and schema validation
public class TenantPipeline {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PipelineOptions options = PipelineOptionsFactory.fromArgs(args).create();
        Pipeline pipeline = Pipeline.create(options);

        pipeline
            .apply("Read from Pub/Sub", PubsubIO.readMessagesWithAttributes()
                .fromSubscription(options.getSubscription()))
            .apply("Extract tenant ID", ParDo.of(new ExtractTenantDoFn()))
            .apply("Validate against schema", ParDo.of(new SchemaValidationDoFn()))
            .apply("Write to tenant BigQuery", new TenantBigQueryWrite());

        pipeline.run();
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design complex, scalable pipelines with dynamic schemas, per-tenant isolation, and robust rollback. Evaluates integration of Pub/Sub, Dataflow, BigQuery, Data Catalog, and access controls, plus testing and lineage practices.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic schema versioning and canonicalization
- Per-tenant access controls (Authorized Views)
- Schema evolution and backfill strategies
- End-to-end data lineage and contracts

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo Beam map to canonical schema (high level)
PCollection<Event> events = pubsub.readEvents("projects/.../topics/...");
events.apply(ParDo.of(new DoFn<EncryptedEvent, CanonicalEvent>() {
  @ProcessElement
  public void process(@Element EncryptedEvent e, OutputReceiver<CanonicalEvent> out) {
    CanonicalEvent c = mapToCanonical(e); // uses tenant-specific schema_version
    out.output(c);
  }
}));
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test schema drift without production impact?
- How would you implement data contracts in Data Catalog and enforce them at ingestion?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical observability and data reliability skills in a real-world, beginner-friendly setting.

## Key Concepts
- Beam Metrics and Cloud Monitoring integration
- Idempotent streaming writes with insertId
- Lightweight auditing and error handling
- Alerting on data quality metrics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-beam sketch (JavaScript/TypeScript syntax used for illustration)
class ParseEvent extends DoFn {
  @ProcessElement
  process(@Element json, output) {
    const event = JSON.parse(json.image);
    this.ingested.inc();
    // ... validation and transformation
    this.processed.inc();
    output(event);
  }

  @Setup
  setup() {
    this.ingested = Metrics.counter('pipeline', 'ingested_events');
    this.processed = Metrics.counter('pipeline', 'processed_events');
    this.failed = Metrics.counter('pipeline', 'failed_events');
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical Dataflow design for multi-table ingestion, basic validation, and idempotent writes in a beginner context. Includes fault handling (DLQ) and a rollback strategy to backfill.

## Key Concepts

- Dataflow (Beam) fundamentals
- Pub/Sub ingestion and DLQ routing
- BigQuery daily partitions and separate schemas
- Idempotent writes with insertId
- Simple schema evolution and archival

